artifact.self_contained_problem:
Provide a precise, operational definition of a "planetary nebula" that satisfies both of the following requirements simultaneously:

(1) Physical/evolutionary requirement. The definition must require that the object consist of an ionized circumstellar shell of gas and dust whose progenitor was a star evolving off the asymptotic giant branch (AGB), and whose ionizing source is a hot post-AGB star or pre-white-dwarf central star produced by that progenitor.

(2) Discriminatory requirement. The definition must, when applied to observational data (imaging, emission-line spectroscopy, kinematics, photometry, central-star characterization, etc.), reliably exclude each of the following classes of "mimics" that can resemble planetary nebulae in one or more diagnostics:
   (a) emission-line galaxies (e.g., compact H II galaxies, AGN narrow-line regions);
   (b) reflection nebulae;
   (c) H II regions ionized by massive young stars;
   (d) Wolf–Rayet ring nebulae and other massive-star ejecta;
   (e) classical and recurrent novae and their shells;
   (f) supernova remnants;
   (g) young stellar objects and their associated nebulosity (e.g., Herbig–Haro flows, proto-stellar shells);
   (h) symbiotic stars and their ionized circumstellar material.

State the definition as a finite list of necessary and sufficient observational and physical criteria (for example, conditions on the mass and evolutionary state of the central star, on the chemical and kinematic properties of the shell, and on energetics and morphology) such that an object qualifies as a planetary nebula if and only if it satisfies all listed criteria, and prove (or otherwise justify) that the criteria correctly include bona-fide planetary nebulae while excluding every class of mimic listed in (2).

